what was measured, under what conditions, and what was expected beforehand. An instrument that reads
to six decimal places will happily report six decimal places of nonsense if the thing it is clamped to
has shifted. The habit that separates a result from a number is writing down the setup before the
reading, so that a disagreement later can be traced to the setup rather than argued about in the
abstract.

